WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:11.533
This is the chapter's lab: choose the target K and a capacity, and the graph and candidate table recompute.

2
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:19.133
Press Step: A–B and D–E are both 5, the tie goes by name, and A–B (gold) is chosen. cut 18 → 13.

3
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:27.000
Step two: AB–C shows 5 (1+4), two parallel wires added up; another tie, and [A, B] comes before [D].

4
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.733
Step three merges D–E: two clusters, ABC | DE, cut 3.

5
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:41.667
Set the capacity to 2 and jump to the end: AB+C is blocked as over capacity, and it stops at 3 clusters, cut 8, without reaching K.

6
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:50.600
Below, the original page's string comparison for contrast: with the same settings, its first step wrongly picks A–C.

7
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.733
The log lists every step; click any row to jump back to it.

8
00:00:55.733 --> 00:01:01.667
The code lab: your code runs in the browser, with no Python to install.

9
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:08.133
Run the checks first: neither task is written yet, so the result is INCOMPLETE.

10
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:15.267
Task 1: the selection key is a tuple: numbers compare as numbers, and members break ties.

11
00:01:15.267 --> 00:02:03.230
Task 2: the main loop. Stop at K; stop when no candidate is legal, telling the two reasons apart; otherwise merge the best pair.

12
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:11.163
Run the checks: everything passes, including multi-digit weights 10 > 9 > 2 and a disconnected graph.

13
00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:19.163
Below, your own greedy_pair_merge() runs on a new graph, compared step by step with the course engine.

14
00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:27.112
Every step's clusters and cut match the engine.

15
00:02:27.112 --> 00:02:34.245
